# GC187 Head and neck cancer problems: Function and shape <span class="tag" tag-name="Surgery"><span class="smallcaps">Surgery</span></span> <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- Principal problems after H&N surgery:
  - Vision
  - Airway
  - Taste
  - Swallowing
  - Speech - phonation vs articulation
  - External appearance - esp. metastasis is uncommon in H&N cancers, and reasonable life-expectancy, patient may be desfigured for long time
- Principles of H&N surgery:
  - Resection - oncological outcomes w/o causing excessive co-morbidities (i.e. resection margins different for different cancers, see slides)
  - Reconstruction - restore cosmesis and function
- Ladder of construction - from simple to difficult in terms of technical difficulty, but no consideration regarding function and aesthetic:
  - Direct closure -\> skin graft -\> local flap -\> distant flap -\> free flap
